      `the person proves to the satisfaction of the Court that he or she had exercised such diligence to prevent the company from being so used as he or she ought to have exercised having regard to the nature of his or her function in that capacity and to all the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 149A(3) the subsection bars the Court from making a disqualification order against the person` MEANS
    IF    `the person proves to the satisfaction of the Court that the company had been used for purposes against national security or interest without his or her consent or connivance`
      AND `the person proves to the satisfaction of the Court that he or she had exercised such diligence to prevent the company from being so used as he or she ought to have exercised having regard to the nature of his or her function in that capacity and to all the circumstances`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "must not make a disqualification order ... if such person proves ... (a) ...; and (b) ..." bars the order where both are proved and says nothing where they are not

